# Schoolgirl-DESCRIBED — the same subject as schoolgirl.blob, authored the
# opposite way: the plate (docs/dev-notes/refs/schoolgirl-views-ref.png) was
# read once for the brief — sailor uniform, bare midriff, navy pleated skirt,
# white slouch calf boots, dark bob — and PUT DOWN. No blob:rings, no
# blob:measure, no head-profile pass: fitting is the thing this run is
# compared against. The mesh was used ONLY to bake the face decal
# (blob:face-bake off a temporary symlink to refs/schoolgirl-mesh/
# schoolgirl.glb — the same face, since she IS the same character).
#
# WHO SHE IS. The cast is a bestiary — a scheming goblin, an ordinary grunt,
# a gothic widow, a clown, a flesh-zombie — and every one of them belongs to
# the Weird West. She doesn't. She is the EXCHANGE STUDENT FROM THE WRONG
# GENRE: crisp navy/white/red colour blocking and toy-clean surfaces dropped
# into a world of mud, leather and dried blood, and completely unbothered by
# it. That is the brainrot read of "schoolgirl in the Weird West" and it is
# why she earns her slot: everyone else is OF the setting, she is IN it.
#
# THE THREE BEATS that carry her silhouette:
#   1. THE BOB — an oversized glossy dark bell, wider than her shoulders are
#      broad, the one mass you can pick out at arena distance. The measured
#      schoolgirl's hair hugs the skull (it was fitted); this one is a
#      HELMET, deliberately.
#   2. THE RED NECKERCHIEF — the single saturated accent (the widow has her
#      cameo; the exchange student has her scarf), a real knot and real
#      hanging tails, not paint.
#   3. THE SLOUCH BOOTS — white leather collapsed around the calf, wider than
#      the leg inside them, black toe caps. The measured version wears socks
#      and mary-janes (that is what the mesh wears); the plate's white boots
#      are louder and they are hers.
#
# HOW SHE DIFFERS FROM THE MEASURED TWO. They fit her to bands and the torso
# still read as stacked discs until it was rebuilt smooth; this one starts
# smooth — a few tapered bars, heavy overlaps. Proportions are the brief's
# anchors (1.70 m, head about 1/8) held to ±10% and no closer.
#
# HER WHOLE OUTFIT IS CLOTH SHELLS (2026-09-05). Blouse, cap sleeves, skirt,
# sailor collar, scarf and boot shafts are all thin onioned sheets standing
# off the body, with real folded edges (rim=) and domain-warped fold shading
# (warp=). Nothing is painted white or navy any more: the flesh underneath is
# SKIN everywhere, and the garment you see is the sheet.
#
# WHAT THAT COST, and it is the lesson worth carrying to the next character:
# PAINT IS BY NEAREST PRIM, so once a garment has real thickness, any small
# accessory sitting UNDER it stops being geometry and becomes a wide painted
# patch of the sheet above it. The scarf and collar points were authored flush
# against a painted blouse; the moment the blouse became a 7 mm sheet they
# read as a red-and-navy flag across her chest. Every one of them had to be
# lifted PROUD of the new surface — and the chest surface is a ramp (z ~0.091
# at the crop hem, ~0.112 at the bust), so "proud" is a different number at
# every height. A tail that hung past the hem then approached the bare midriff,
# which sits ~40 mm further back, and painted a red band across her stomach.
#
# The rule that falls out: on a shell garment you cannot paint detail, you can
# only stand geometry on it, and a couple of millimetres of clearance is
# enough — a centimetre reads as stuck on.

model schoolgirl-described
  height 1.70
  # humanoid | digitigrade. Checked, not decorative — see blob-checks.ts.
  stance humanoid

skeleton
  # The female/soldier vertical budget: root at 0.85 so the leg chain
  # (0.42 + 0.415 below the pelvis tail at 0.95) lands the ankle at 0.115,
  # legs exactly half the standing height — she is ordinary-human scale like
  # the soldier and the widow.
  root pelvis at 0.85 len=0.10

  # Dead upright. The zombie owns the stoop, the goblin the hunch; she
  # stands like the bell just rang.
  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=0 len=0.15
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=0 len=0.15
  bone spine2 parent=chest  dir=up pitch=0 len=0.06
  bone neck   parent=spine2 dir=up pitch=0 len=0.08
  # Carries the face block: at=0.55 puts the cranium centre at ~1.51, chin
  # ~1.44, flesh crown ~1.60; the bob tops at ~1.69 against the 1.70 pin.
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=2 len=0.22

  mirror
    # Shoulder socket at x ~0.123, y ~1.33. The goblin lesson: shoulder
    # reach is a function of chest width — the blouse yoke here is
    # half-width ~0.095, so 0.028/0.095 lands the socket just proud of it.
    bone clavicle parent=spine2  side=0.028 dir=side  len=0.095
    # Arms hang DOWN and slightly OUT (tilt 9/6) — the schoolgirl measured
    # the <=6 deg fog line where arm and waist melt, and her skirt flares
    # to ~0.17 half-width at the hem; the hands must land outside it.
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down tilt=9 pitch=2  len=0.24
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down tilt=6 pitch=10 len=0.21
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down pitch=8         len=0.09
    # Legs: dead straight with a whisper of outward slant, the soldier's
    # at-ease stand scaled down. No knee-together shyness — that was a
    # measured fact about the mesh's stance, not a choice.
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis dir=down tilt=2 side=0.058 len=0.42
    bone shin     parent=thigh  dir=down tilt=2            len=0.415
    # The foot bone only positions the boot prims (the house pattern).
    bone foot     parent=shin   dir=fwd                    len=0.12
  end

body
  # ============================================================
  # TORSO — bare skin, all of it. Every mass here is her BODY; the uniform is
  # cloth shells laid over it further down this file. Few large tapered bars,
  # heavy overlaps — the smooth-start the measured one's disc-stack rebuild
  # proved. Skin warm pale (see the face block), blouse white f2f2f5, navy
  # 0b1278, scarf red b01018.
  # ============================================================
  # Hips under the skirt — skin; never seen, keeps the legs fused.
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.45 r=0.098 wide=1.00 tall=0.95 deep=0.84 blend=0.008 color=e9c9ae
  # Waist: bar up spine1 (y 1.00 -> 1.10), cinched to 0.060 at the top —
  # she is slim; the bare midriff band runs 1.05-1.14 between the
  # waistband and the blouse hem.
  bar  torso on spine1 from=0.35 to=1.00 r=0.084 r2=0.060 deep=0.78 blend=0.006 color=e9c9ae
  # The top of the midriff, where the blouse hem will cross it.
  bar  torso on chest  from=0.00 to=0.25 r=0.060 r2=0.066 deep=0.85 blend=0.006 color=e9c9ae
  # THE BLOUSE: one tapered bar from the midriff overlap up to the bust.
  # The paint boundary with the midriff bar lands ~1.13 — the crop hem is
  # a colour change on a smooth surface, not a ledge.
  bar  torso on chest  from=0.18 to=0.85 r=0.064 r2=0.096 deep=0.95 blend=0.008 color=e9c9ae
  # Bust mounds, front — the side-view read of the blouse, as geometry.
  blob torso on chest  at=0.60 offset=(0.042,0,0.050) r=0.052 deep=1.00 blend=0.006 both color=e9c9ae
  # Yoke: chest top to the neck, thin front-to-back — the collar cape
  # drapes over this.
  bar  torso on spine2 from=0.00 to=0.90 r=0.078 r2=0.046 deep=0.80 blend=0.006 color=e9c9ae

  # ============================================================
  # THE MIDDY BLOUSE — cloth now, not paint. A sheet standing ~7 mm off the
  # torso from the crop hem up to the shoulders, so the hem is a real folded
  # edge (rim=) instead of a colour change on smooth skin, and the fabric
  # carries fold shading of its own. The flesh under it is SKIN: the white
  # you see is the shell, and nothing is painted white any more.
  # ============================================================
  shell torso on chest at=0.15 tip=(0,0.118,0) r=0.073 r2=0.103 wide=1.05 deep=1.06 thick=0.005 warp=0.007 warpFreq=(24,10,24) clip=(0,-1,0) clipd=-1.130 rim=0.006 blend=0.005 color=f2f2f5

  # PUFFED CAP SLEEVES — the same trick on the arm: a short cone sheet cut at
  # the cuff, so the sleeve ends in a folded edge standing clear of the bare
  # arm inside it rather than a paint boundary running across it.
  shell arm on upperarm at=0.06 tip=(0.010,-0.078,0) r=0.036 r2=0.031 wide=1.06 deep=1.06 thick=0.005 warp=0.004 warpFreq=(40,18,40) clip=(0,-1,0) clipd=-1.205 rim=0.005 blend=0.005 mirror color=f2f2f5

  # ============================================================
  # THE SKIRT — one flared shell cone, navy, hem at mid-thigh. Cloth, not
  # mass (the house pattern): a thin onioned surface, clipped at the hem
  # with a rounded rim so the edge reads as folded cloth. Waistband 0.072
  # half at y 1.05 flaring to 0.175 at the hem (y 0.74) — a stronger A-line
  # than the measured version's, because the flare is doing the pleats'
  # job: no grooves, no pleat fragments, colour and silhouette only.
  # ============================================================
  # CLOTH FOLDS, not pleats — and the distinction is the spike's finding.
  # warp=0.016 at warpFreq=(20,0,20): the zero on Y freezes that sine to a
  # constant so the folds run vertically rather than dimpling the skirt, and
  # the hem picks up a soft scallop instead of reading as a cut cone.
  #
  # It is NOT a pleated skirt and cannot be made into one. The warp is a
  # product of Cartesian sines, so it is periodic on a GRID, not around the
  # body: it vanishes near the centre front and centre back (where sin(Fx*x)
  # crosses zero) and is strongest on the diagonals. Real pleats are periodic
  # in the ANGLE around the waist, which this function cannot express. Pushed
  # to warp=0.026 the asymmetry stops reading as fabric weight and starts
  # reading as a skirt someone slept in — tried, and backed off from.
  #
  # 0.016 * length(20,0,20) = 0.45, so the march takes 45% shorter steps
  # wherever the skirt is on screen. Under the pinch cap of 1.
  shell torso on spine1 at=0.580 r=0.072 r2=0.175 tip=(0,-0.300,0) wide=1.06 tall=0.38 deep=0.80 thick=0.006 warp=0.016 warpFreq=(20,0,20) clip=(0,-1,0) clipd=-0.740 rim=0.008 blend=0.006 color=0b1278

  # ============================================================
  # SAILOR COLLAR — cloth, not mass: a thin navy cape lying on the
  # shoulders and back (the front clipped open so the blouse shows), plus
  # the two front points riding the bust down-inward to the scarf knot.
  # ============================================================
  # The cape drapes rather than pleats — a soft isotropic sag at a LOW
  # frequency, so the navy reads as a cloth lying on the shoulders instead of
  # a painted shoulder plate. 0.005 * length(14,14,14) = 0.12.
  shell torso on chest at=0.78 offset=(0,0.035,-0.015) r=0.112 wide=1.00 tall=0.95 deep=1.02 thick=0.006 warp=0.006 warpFreq=13 clip=(0,0,1) clipd=0.030 rim=0.005 blend=0.006 color=0b1278
  shell torso on chest at=0.94 offset=(0.050,0.020,0.066) tip=(-0.019,-0.076,0.036) r=0.012 r2=0.004 wide=0.90 tall=0.75 deep=0.50 thick=0.004 clip=(0,0,1) clipd=0.300 rim=0.003 blend=0.0035 both color=0b1278

  # ============================================================
  # THE RED NECKERCHIEF (beat 2) — a real knot at the V and real tails
  # hanging down the blouse front, proud of the chest. The mesh's scarf is
  # red (the measured control's was navy — its own test calls that out);
  # red is also the one accent the cast's colour grammar understands.
  # ============================================================
  blob torso on chest at=0.78 offset=(0,-0.004,0.112) r=0.009 wide=1.25 tall=0.85 deep=0.55 blend=0.003 color=b01018
  shell torso on chest at=0.76 offset=(0,-0.010,0.110) tip=(0.002,-0.050,-0.016) r=0.009 r2=0.005 wide=1.10 deep=0.38 thick=0.004 warp=0.004 warpFreq=(30,26,30) clip=(0,-1,0) clipd=-1.150 rim=0.003 blend=0.003 color=b01018

  # Neck: slim, skin, between the collar and the jaw.
  bar head on neck from=0.10 to=0.90 r=0.030 deep=1.15 blend=0.004 color=e9c9ae

  # ============================================================
  # THE BOB (beat 1) — five prims, and yes, that is the same hair budget
  # the measured schoolgirl spent: a bob IS a mass haircut and the masses
  # are the honest way to build it. The dome is deliberately oversized —
  # a helmet of hair, wider than the skull under it (the face block is
  # 0.072 half-wide; the lobes reach 0.108).
  #   dome  — crown + back, tops at ~1.69 against the 1.70 pin.
  #   lobes — the bell flare below the crown, framing the cheeks open.
  #   fringe — the brow rim; the DECAL paints the true hairline and the
  #            widow's peak, so geometry stays at the temple line (the
  #            measured one's double-hairline lesson).
  #   nape  — closes the bob's underside at the neck.
  #   locks — two pointed side locks in front of the ears, the plate's
  #           face-framing strands. These are the ONE place strands would
  #           beat masses; see the report re: hairlock.
  # ============================================================
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0,0.070,-0.022) r=0.100 wide=1.06 tall=0.94 deep=1.00 blend=0.006 color=2a1a10 gloss=0.25
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0.072,-0.045,-0.025) r=0.036 wide=1.00 tall=1.50 deep=2.00 blend=0.006 both color=2a1a10 gloss=0.30
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0,0.054,0.044) r=0.048 wide=1.35 tall=0.72 deep=0.90 blend=0.004 color=2a1a10 gloss=0.22
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0,-0.058,-0.058) r=0.045 wide=1.05 tall=0.95 deep=1.00 blend=0.005 color=2a1a10 gloss=0.30
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0.055,-0.005,0.050) tip=(0.004,-0.095,0.004) r=0.014 r2=0.004 blend=0.003 chamfer both color=2a1a10 gloss=0.22
  # Chin: a small definite point, blended soft so it reads as the jaw's
  # tip, not a bead.
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0,-0.105,0.062) r=0.020 wide=1.15 tall=0.60 deep=0.90 blend=0.004 color=e9c9ae

  # ============================================================
  # ARMS — slim rubber-hose, no ball joints, BARE the whole way: the puffed
  # sleeve is a shell up with the blouse, so the arm under it runs unbroken
  # from shoulder to mitt. It used to stop at 0.40 because the white sleeve
  # bar WAS the upper arm; deleting the paint without extending this would
  # have left her shoulders hollow inside a sheet.
  # ============================================================
  blob arm on clavicle at=1.00 offset=(0,-0.020,0) r=0.026 tall=0.85 blend=0.005 mirror core color=e9c9ae
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.05 to=0.98 r=0.027 r2=0.022 blend=0.0045 mirror color=e9c9ae
  blob arm on forearm  at=0.00 r=0.022 blend=0.0045 mirror color=e9c9ae
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.05 to=0.95 r=0.021 r2=0.018 blend=0.004 mirror color=e9c9ae
  blob arm on hand at=0.50 offset=(0,0,0.015) r=0.024 wide=0.85 tall=1.10 deep=1.20 blend=0.004 mirror color=e9c9ae

  # ============================================================
  # LEGS — bare skin from the skirt hem to the boot tops; slim (the plate
  # carries no calf bulge, the boot does the bulging).
  # ============================================================
  blob leg on thigh at=0.00 r=0.058 deep=1.05 blend=0.006 mirror core color=e9c9ae
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.02 to=1.00 r=0.060 r2=0.042 deep=1.05 blend=0.005 mirror color=e9c9ae
  blob leg on shin  at=0.00 r=0.042 deep=1.05 blend=0.005 mirror color=e9c9ae
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.02 to=0.99 r=0.042 r2=0.030 deep=0.95 blend=0.005 mirror color=e9c9ae

  # ============================================================
  # THE SLOUCH BOOTS (beat 3) — the SHAFT is a shell now: a sheet standing
  # clear of the bare calf, with a folded top edge, which is what a slouch
  # boot actually is. The shin bar under it had to be extended to the ankle
  # for the same reason the arm did — the old white bar WAS the lower leg.
  # The cuff roll and the foot stay solid: a fold-over roll is a mass, and a
  # foot is not cloth. Black toe
  # caps and tread. This is where she parts from the measured version's
  # socks-and-mary-janes: the boot is the bigger, dumber, more readable
  # shape, and white boots at the bottom answer the white blouse at the
  # top with the navy skirt between — the whole outfit in three blocks.
  # ============================================================
  shell leg on shin at=0.33 tip=(0,-0.268,0) r=0.062 r2=0.052 wide=1.02 deep=0.88 thick=0.006 warp=0.005 warpFreq=(34,0,34) clip=(0,-1,0) clipd=-0.126 rim=0.006 blend=0.005 mirror color=f2f2f5 gloss=0.12
  blob leg on shin  at=0.38 r=0.064 deep=0.82 blend=0.006 mirror color=f2f2f5 gloss=0.12
  # Boot foot + heel wrap, world-displacement off the foot bone (the house
  # pattern); its tread reaches the floor (centre y 0.06, half-tall 0.058).
  blob leg on foot at=0.00 r=0.048 wide=1.25 tall=1.20 deep=1.30 blend=0.006 offset=(0,-0.055,0.010) mirror color=f2f2f5 gloss=0.12
  # Black toe cap: toe yawed out a whisper, tread at y ~0.003.
  blob leg on foot at=0.00 r=0.030 r2=0.025 wide=1.60 tall=1.00 deep=1.10 blend=0.006 offset=(0.008,-0.082,-0.035) tip=(0.012,0.000,0.100) mirror color=17110c gloss=0.20

# ============================================================
# FACE BLOCK — a small oval skull under the bob: narrow jaw, light brow,
# a button nose. The face ITSELF is the baked decal below; painted-prim
# features have read as a visor band on three dispatches, so none here.
# ============================================================
face
  headRadius 0.072
  headWidth  0.94
  headHeight 1.28
  headDepth  1.258
  jawWidth   0.78
  jawHeight  0.74
  jawDrop    0.058
  jawJut     0.017
  noseLength 0.037
  noseWidth  0.936
  noseDrop   0.054
  browHeavy  0.0
  browRise   0.0
  headBlend  0.005

# Her face, baked flat off the mesh's head (blob:face-bake; the script
# resolves refs/<name>-mesh/<name>.glb, which for this character was a
# temporary symlink to schoolgirl.glb — she IS the same character, so she
# wears the same face). 0.34/0.40, NOT the bake's printed 0.19/0.237: the
# print is generic, and this bake is IDENTICAL to the schoolgirl's (same
# mesh, same head band), whose tuned solve converged at 0.38/0.42 — this
# skull is ~8% narrower, so the image sits a touch larger. projScale is a
# FREQUENCY, lower = bigger; the 0.45/0.58 defaults are for the GENERATED
# sheet. decal 0 = multiply, so the face takes the body's light.
sheet
  image       schoolgirl-described-face.png
  decal       0
  projScaleX  0.34
  projScaleY  0.44
  projCentreX 0.50
  projCentreY 0.57
  # Below the bake's max luma or nothing glows (the soldier lesson: his
  # bake tops at 0.855 and the default 0.88 cut gave him dead eyes).
  eyeGlowCut  0.7
  texRelief   1.400
  texStrength 1.000
  eyeGlowAmp  0.4

# Skin e9c9ae = sRGB (233,201,174) -> linear (0.815, 0.586, 0.432): warm
# and light, a touch more sun than the widow's porcelain, none of the
# measured version's orange. Toy-clean: no mottle to speak of, moderate
# gloss. Wounds stay red.
palette
  baseColor     0.815 0.586 0.432
  deepColor     0.50 0.08 0.08
  charColor     0.22 0.13 0.10
  specIntensity 0.42
  specRoughness 0.38
  fresnelBoost  0.28
  translucency  0.22
  surfaceNoiseAmp    0.0
  silhouetteNoiseAmp 0.0
  wetness       0.10
  mottleAmp     0.02
  mottleScale   1.2
  mottleColor   0.55 0.38 0.24
